/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  flash23_gas_only_oh_quench  --  the witness of the GAS-ONLY phase rule
  (DEV.md 4c C458, 2026-11-21).

  A hot combustion gas (N2, water and a trace of the hydroxyl radical OH)
  goes through two isothermal flashes at 1 bar:

    hot    1500 K  -- above every dew point: one vapour, nothing to split;
    cold    330 K  -- below water's dew point: water condenses.

  OH is NOT a component: it is a gas-only species of the NASA table
  (`role gasOnly;`), resolved by name because the property system declares
  `speciesTables ( NASA-TP-2002-211556 );`.  Gas-only means it has no
  condensed phase and no data for one, so in every phase split its K is the
  non-condensable cap ThermoPackage::kNoLiquidK = 1e12: all of it leaves in
  the vapour, and the condensate carries at most z/1e12 of it.  Until C458
  a radical was `role nonvolatile;` (K = 0) and the same cold flash sent
  every OH molecule into the water.

  FROZEN COMPOSITION, ON PURPOSE.  No reaction runs: in a real quench OH
  recombines in microseconds, and that is chemistry (a mechanism's job,
  tutorials/batch/combustion).  This case asks only the phase question.

  The refusal half -- the same feed declared as a SATURATED LIQUID, a state
  with no vapour to hold OH, refused by name -- is held by the gate
  bin/curate/check_gas_only.py, which edits a probe copy of this case.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

units
(
    {
        name        hot;
        type        isothermalFlash;
        model       rachfordRice;
        in          feed;
        outputs     ( hotLiquid  hotVapour );

        operation   { T 1500.0 K;  P 1.0 bar; }
    }
    {
        name        cold;
        type        isothermalFlash;
        model       rachfordRice;
        in          hotVapour;
        outputs     ( condensate  offGas );

        operation   { T 330.0 K;  P 1.0 bar; }
    }
);
